{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f41bd9c\" lines=\"10-30\">\n<description>\nMove the side-attached foreground object to the opposite wall, shear its lower rows inward, and paint the crossed floor cells.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is the upper background color and the bottom row is the\n  floor color.\n- A single non-background, non-floor object touches exactly one vertical\n  grid edge and rests immediately above the first solid floor row.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the source object to the upper background color.\n- Repaint each object row on the opposite side: the first three relative\n  rows keep the edge alignment, and each lower row shifts one more pixel\n  inward from that side.\n- On the first floor row, repaint the traversed side-to-object segment\n  maroon, stopping just before the final outer bottom support pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The side of the source object gives the horizontal direction, while the\n  row index inside the object gives the one-cell-per-row diagonal shear.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"36a08778\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nSimulate water falling from top-edge sources, pooling on shelves, and cascading off edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; water = rarest non-bg; shelf = other non-bg.\n- Sources: water clusters touching top row. Shelves: horizontal segments.\n\nRule:\n- Water falls down from sources, painting bg cells as water.\n- On hitting a shelf, it spreads across the shelf span plus one cell past each end.\n- From those overflow positions, water resumes falling independently.\n\nKey insight: Each shelf splits a stream into two at its edges, creating a branching waterfall tree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"98c475bf\" lines=\"62-76\">\n<description>\nErase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.\n- Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.\n- Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.\n\nRule\n- Keep the vertical frame unchanged.\n- Erase any complete interior glyph.\n- For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.\n\nKey insight\n- Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n\nRule\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n\nKey insight\n- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"79cce52d\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nCrop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.\n- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.\n- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top control row and left control column.\n- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.\n- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.\n\nKey insight\n- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nRecover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n\nRule:\n- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n\nKey insight:\nThe hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cc9053aa\" lines=\"59-72\">\n<description>\nRecolor the unique safe route between the two maroon endpoints from teal to maroon.\n\nInput structure\n- A black background contains a teal 4-connected track, exactly two maroon endpoint pixels, and one or more orange blocker pixels embedded near the track.\n\nRule\n- Treat each orange blocker and its 8-neighborhood as forbidden.\n- In the remaining teal/maroon track graph, there is exactly one simple\n  4-connected path between the maroon endpoints.\n- Recolor teal pixels on that path to maroon; leave endpoints, blockers, and all other cells unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The orange pixels block nearby teal cells diagonally as well as orthogonally, which removes shorter decoy routes and makes the intended endpoint-to-endpoint path unique.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3f79277\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nReplace a corner L with a same-color right-triangle outline in the corresponding corner of an enlarged canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a square grid with one background color and one foreground color.\n- The foreground is a one-pixel-thick L inside a square bbox.\n- The L touches exactly one horizontal grid edge and one vertical grid edge, identifying a corner.\n\nRule:\n- Fill a square output canvas of side 3n-2, where n is the input side length, with the background color.\n- Double the L leg length to get the triangle side length.\n- Draw the two perpendicular triangle legs on the same output corner edges and connect their free endpoints with the diagonal.\n\nKey insight:\n- The input L encodes the corner, color, and side length of the triangle outline; the missing side is the diagonal between the L's two free endpoints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaef0977\" lines=\"25-41\">\n<description>\nReplace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.\n- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.\n\nRule:\n- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.\n- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position\n  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.\n- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.\n\nKey insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the\ndistance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that\nexcludes the orange background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
